    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Right unit / cohesion.** The class's single responsibility \u2014 \"assign the owner's `customerCode`\" \u2014 is preserved. Uniqueness is intrinsic to that field's assignment, so folding de-duplication into `AssignCustomerCode` keeps the logic where a maintainer would look for it rather than fragmenting one field's derivation across two steps (which would also incur `Out<T>`/`@Val` wiring to pass the base code along).\n\n- **Matches the established sibling pattern.** `CountNamesakes`, `FlagPossibleDuplicate`, and `EnsureUniqueHousehold` all inject `OwnerRepository`, iterate `ownerRepository.findAll()` over existing owners, and stamp a field on the `@Val Owner`. The reviewed change does exactly this, so the new `OwnerRepository` dependency and the `findAll()` scan are entirely in keeping with the conventions. Using a `HashSet` for the membership check in the de-dup loop is a sensible, minor local refinement over the inline-match style, appropriate because this step tests containment repeatedly.\n\n- **No catch-all / no duplication.** The class still does one thing (compute + uniquify one code); the pure `sha256UpperHex8` helper remains private and focused. There's no shared \"scan existing owners\" helper being reinvented \u2014 each sibling rolls its own `findAll` loop, so this is consistent, not duplicative.\n\n- **Documentation.** The javadoc was updated coherently: the stale \"There are no sequence numbers\" line was removed and a dedicated paragraph documents the collision/append-`-<n>` behavior, matching the spec and the sibling classes' doc style.\n\n(Per instructions I did not assess correctness \u2014 e.g. the loop semantics or the `findAll` cost \u2014 only structure.)",
